.\" sysv / xenix driver man page
.TH SYSV 4 "2026-05-13" "Substrate Kernel" "Special Files"
.SH NAME
sysv, xenix \- read-only mount driver for V7-derived filesystems
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mount -t sysv
.I device mountpoint
.br
.B mount -t xenix
.I device mountpoint
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B sysv
driver provides
.B read-only
mount support for the V7-derived family of on-disk layouts:
.IP \[bu] 2
.B Xenix
(Microsoft Xenix and SCO Xenix 86 / 286 / 386 \(em V7 filesystem with the
0x012FF7B5 superblock magic).
.IP \[bu]
.B System V
release 2 / 3 / 4 (0xFD187E20 superblock magic).
.IP \[bu]
.B Coherent
(no magic; recognised only when explicitly requested).
.PP
The driver is the same code path regardless of which name (\fBsysv\fR or
\fBxenix\fR) was used at mount time; both names exist to make the intent obvious
when reading a
.IR /etc/fstab
or
.BR mount (8)
invocation.
.SS On-disk format
The superblock is a 1024-byte block at disk offset 512.  Magic and "type" words
sit at offsets 0x1F8 and 0x1FC respectively:
.TP
.B Magic
0x012FF7B5 means Xenix; 0xFD187E20 means SystemV; anything else is rejected.
.TP
.B Type
1 = 512-byte logical blocks (Xenix V1 only), 2 = 1024-byte (Xenix V2 and
SystemV), 3 = 2048-byte (SystemV "AFS").
.PP
Each inode is 64 bytes; the root inode is number 2 (V7 convention).  Direct
and indirect block pointers in
.B i_addr[]
are packed as 13 three-byte little-endian zone numbers in 40 bytes \(em the
high byte sits one above the low two bytes, just like the historical V7 disk
encoding.  The driver unpacks these at inode-read time and caches the resulting
32-bit block numbers in the per-node state.
.SS Features
.IP \[bu] 2
Variants: Xenix V1 (512-byte block), Xenix V2 / SystemV (1024-byte block),
SystemV AFS (2048-byte block).
.IP \[bu]
Directory traversal:
.BR readdir (3)
and
.BR finddir (3)
through V7-style 16-byte
.I struct sysv_dirent
records (14-byte name field).
.IP \[bu]
File data: direct, single-indirect, double-indirect, and triple-indirect zone
addressing.  Sparse files (zero zone numbers) read back as zeros, matching V7
semantics.
.SS Limitations
.IP \[bu] 2
Read-only.  All write paths (write, mkdir, unlink, rename, truncate) are
unimplemented; attempts return
.BR EROFS .
.IP \[bu]
14-character filenames only.  Longer-name Xenix variants from SCO Unix-derived
systems are not yet probed for.
.IP \[bu]
Coherent variant is wired into the variant enum but not auto-probed.
.SH EXAMPLE
Mount an SCO Xenix 86 disk image at
.IR /mnt/xenix :
.PP
.RS
.nf
mount -t xenix /dev/storage/ide1 /mnt/xenix
.fi
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I sys/fs/sysv/sysv.c
Driver implementation.
.TP
.I sys/fs/sysv/sysv.h
On-disk structure definitions and per-mount state.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mount (8),
.BR mount (2),
.BR ext2 (4),
.BR udf (5)
.SH HISTORY
The V7 filesystem layout dates to UNIX V7 (1979).  Microsoft Xenix carried it
forward to the 8086 / 80286 / 80386 in the early 1980s; SCO Xenix 86 and later
SCO Xenix System V both ship variants of this format.  System V release 2
introduced the 1024-byte block size and the AFS extensions added 2048-byte
blocks for performance.
